# sway config — managed by dfinstall (modules/sway.go), linked to ~/.config/sway/config
#
# ############################################################################
# #  RULE 1: $mod IS Mod4 (Super). THERE ARE NO Alt (Mod1) BINDINGS HERE.    #
# #                                                                          #
# #  tmux owns the entire Alt keyspace on this box: prefix M-a, plus         #
# #  no-prefix binds on M-1..M-9, M-n, M-Enter, M--, M-q, M-m, M-s, M-c.    #
# #  sway grabs keys BEFORE the terminal sees them, so any Alt binding here  #
# #  silently steals a tmux binding. Do not add one. Ever.                   #
# ############################################################################
#
# Validate before logging in — a broken config leaves you at a black screen:
#   sway --validate -c config/sway/config
# Off a graphical session that needs a backend hint:
#   env WLR_BACKENDS=headless sway --validate -c config/sway/config

set $mod Mod4
set $left  h
set $down  j
set $up    k
set $right l

set $term    ghostty
set $menu    fuzzel
set $lock    swaylock -f -c 1e1e2e

### Appearance -------------------------------------------------------------
# Matches config/waybar/style.css and config/ghostty/config — the fonts module
# installs this family, so keep the three in step. NOT "Hack": that family was
# never installed here (only "Hack Nerd Font" ever was, which is a different
# family name), so `pango:Hack` silently fell through to the generic monospace.
font pango:IosevkaTerm Nerd Font 10

# Titlebars off. Revert with `normal 2`.
#
# ⚠ THE WINDOW TITLE IS NOT DISPLAYED ANYWHERE ON THIS DESKTOP, and this comment
# has now been wrong twice by claiming otherwise. It used to read "the title is
# already in waybar's center pill" — true when `modules-center` was
# `["sway/window"]`, but the center pill now holds the CLOCK
# (config/waybar/config), so there is no title anywhere: not in a titlebar, not
# in the bar. That is deliberate, not an oversight. To get it back, put
# "sway/window" into modules-center and move the clock to modules-right.
#
# NOTE: tabbed ($mod+w) and stacking ($mod+s) layouts still draw titlebars
# regardless of this setting — that is why the client.* titlebar background
# and text colors below still matter.
default_border          pixel 2
default_floating_border pixel 2

# ⚠ THE FOCUSED BORDER IS HIDDEN WHEN A WORKSPACE HOLDS ONE WINDOW, and that is
# a deliberate trade — read this before removing the line.
#
# What it fixes: with a single window the 2px client.focused border (#89b4fa,
# plus the #f5e0dc indicator on the split edge) drew a bright blue frame around
# the whole screen — 2876 border pixels along the top edge alone. Against the
# swaync control center, whose outer edge is deliberately square and quiet
# (config/swaync/style.css), that frame was the loudest thing on screen and read
# as an outline bolted onto the panel. Measured 2876 -> 0 with this line.
#
# Why `smart` and not `default_border none`: borders still appear the moment a
# workspace holds 2+ windows, which is the case where they earn their keep by
# showing which pane has focus. Nothing is lost while tiling.
#
# ⚠ WHAT IT COSTS, and this comment used to sit above `default_border` claiming
# the opposite: on a MULTI-MONITOR layout with one window per output there is now
# no border anywhere, so the focused border can no longer tell you which SCREEN
# has focus. That cue is gone by choice — owen's call, chosen over both dimming
# client.focused and dropping borders entirely. If it turns out to matter, the
# non-destructive alternative is to keep this line off and instead recolor
# client.focused's border+indicator to a dark surface tone (#313244), which
# quiets the frame without hiding anything.
#
# ⚠ `--i3` MATTERS. Without it sway uses its own semantics for `smart`; the i3
# flag is the "hide when only one window is visible" behavior intended here.
# `smart_no_gaps` is NOT the right value: it additionally requires gaps to be
# zero, and `gaps inner 8` / `outer 4` below mean it would never trigger.
hide_edge_borders --i3 smart

# ⚠ smart_gaps IS OFF, AND THIS COMMENT HAS NOW ARGUED BOTH WAYS — do not read
# an earlier revision as authority. It was OFF (gaps always), then ON ("alone =
# full bleed"), and is OFF again as of 2026-08-09. The current intent is that a
# lone window is framed exactly like a tiled one: same 10px gaps, same rounded
# corners, so splitting a workspace does not restyle the window you were
# already looking at.
#
# ⚠ THE "ALONE = FULL BLEED" SET IS DISSOLVED. Two of its three settings moved
# together here; the third is deliberately left alone:
#   · smart_gaps off             here                    — gaps always
#   · smart_corner_radius disable  config/sway/sway-fx   — rounded always
#   · hide_edge_borders --i3 smart  above  — LEFT ON, and it is not part of this
#     any more. It drops the BORDER on a lone window, which is an independent
#     aesthetic call owen already made (see its own comment); gaps do not
#     re-justify a bright #89b4fa frame around a single window.
#
# smart_corner_radius had exactly one job — a window flush to the screen edge
# cannot have rounded corners without cutting visible notches out of the display
# corners. With gaps always on, nothing is ever flush, so the job is gone. Do
# NOT re-enable it without also setting smart_gaps back on; alone it just
# squares the corners of a window that has room to be round.
#
# `inverse_outer` is the other value worth knowing and is NOT what is wanted: it
# applies outer gaps ONLY when a workspace has exactly one child, i.e. it would
# give a lone window MORE gap than a tiled one.
gaps inner 8
# ⚠ PER-EDGE, AND THE TOP IS NEGATIVE ON PURPOSE. Outer gaps are IN ADDITION to
# inner, so the visible gap at an edge is inner + that edge's outer.
#
# The target is that every visible gap is the same 10px, including the one
# between a window and the bar — and that spawning a second window does not
# move anything. Before this:
#
#         left/right/bottom   12   (inner 8 + outer 4)
#         window vs bar pill  22   (bar's own margin-bottom and the pill's
#                                   CSS margin sit inside the exclusive zone,
#                                   and the outer gap stacked on top of them)
#         one window          10   (smart_gaps, flush to the zone)
#
# so the gap above a window JUMPED FROM 10 TO 22 the moment a second window
# spawned, which is the "feels off when spawned" — and windows sat at x=12
# while the bar's pills start at x=10, 2px out of line with them.
#
# outer 2 makes the three screen-edge gaps 10, which is exactly the bar's own
# margin-left/right, so windows now line up with the pills above them.
# top -8 cancels the inner gap at the top entirely, because the air there is
# ALREADY provided by the bar's margin-bottom (see config/waybar/config) — the
# outer gap was double-counting it. Windows now start at y=52 whether there is
# one of them or nine.
#
# ⚠ These four are a set with waybar's `margin-bottom` and `margin-left/right`.
# Change one and re-measure the others; the alignment is the point, not the
# individual numbers.
gaps outer 2
gaps top -8
smart_gaps off

### Colors ----------------------------------------------------------------
# Catppuccin Mocha, the same palette as config/waybar/style.css — keep the two
# in step. Before this the config set no client.* directives at all, so sway
# fell back to its stock #4c7899/#285577 blue-gray, which is what made the
# borders clash with the bar.
#
# Dark titlebars with the accent carried by the border and the title text,
# mirroring the bar's "accent text on dark" treatment rather than filling the
# titlebar with color.
#
#                       border   background text     indicator child_border
client.focused          #89b4fa  #181825    #89b4fa  #f5e0dc   #89b4fa
client.focused_inactive #45475a  #181825    #a6adc8  #45475a   #45475a
client.unfocused        #313244  #181825    #6c7086  #313244   #313244
client.urgent           #f38ba8  #181825    #f38ba8  #f38ba8   #f38ba8
client.placeholder      #313244  #181825    #6c7086  #313244   #313244
client.background       #1e1e2e

# $HOME rather than a literal path: sway expands it here, and `sway --validate`
# genuinely checks the file exists, so a broken path fails loudly at validate
# time rather than silently leaving you on the default gray.
output * bg $HOME/.config/background fill

### Outputs ----------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ Output geometry is HOST-SPECIFIC and deliberately NOT in this file.
#
# A sway output is matched by IDENTIFIER ("make model serial"), which embeds
# each panel's serial number — so an output block is per-machine data, not
# shareable config. Keep it in ~/.config/sway/outputs.conf.
# See config/sway/outputs.conf.example for the shape and the full reasoning.
#
# A missing include is silently tolerated by sway (verified: `--validate`
# exits 0 and says nothing), so a machine without an outputs.conf just gets
# sway's default auto-placement rather than an error.
#
# Why identifiers and not connector names — the one durable lesson worth
# keeping here: a DisplayLink/evdi output's connector is assigned in attach
# order, so it enumerates as DVI-I-1 one session and DVI-I-2 the next, and
# `output DVI-I-1 ...` silently stops applying. Identifiers survive replugs.
# Get them with:  swaymsg -t get_outputs | grep -E 'make|model|serial'
include $HOME/.config/sway/outputs.conf

### Input ------------------------------------------------------------------
# "Don't reject the mouse": click-to-focus, not follow-mouse.
focus_follows_mouse no
mouse_warping      output

input type:touchpad {
    tap enabled
    natural_scroll enabled
    dwt enabled
    middle_emulation enabled
    scroll_factor 0.6
}

input type:keyboard {
    xkb_layout us
    repeat_delay 250
    repeat_rate 40
}

# $mod + drag to move, $mod + right-drag to resize.
floating_modifier $mod normal

### Launchers --------------------------------------------------------------
bindsym $mod+Return exec $term
bindsym $mod+d      exec $menu
# ⚠ The ONLY Alt binding in this file, and a deliberate exception to RULE 1
# above (PowerToys Run muscle memory). Mod1+space is safe because tmux binds
# Alt only on  a n 1-9 Enter - q m s c  and the mouse, so space is free;
# Super+space was unavailable (it is `focus mode_toggle` below).
# Do NOT add further Alt bindings; every other Alt key risks a silent steal.
# Before adding one, check both sides:
#   grep -E 'bind.*M-' config/tmux/tmux.conf
#   tmux list-keys -T root | grep 'M-'
bindsym Mod1+space  exec $menu
bindsym $mod+e      exec nautilus
# Close the focused window. $mod+Shift+q is the i3/sway default and stays bound
# for muscle memory; $mod+q is the same command, one key shorter.
#
# ⚠ Focused, not hovered — and on this box those differ, because
# `focus_follows_mouse no` above is deliberate. A keyboard binding cannot target
# the hovered window: sway exposes no pointer coordinates over IPC, so there is
# nothing for a helper to query. Mouse bindings are the exception (they act on
# the container under the pointer), so hover-close, if ever wanted, is
# `bindsym --whole-window $mod+button2 kill` beside `floating_modifier`.
bindsym $mod+q       kill
bindsym $mod+Shift+q kill

### Focus / movement -------------------------------------------------------
bindsym $mod+$left  focus left
bindsym $mod+$down  focus down
bindsym $mod+$up    focus up
bindsym $mod+$right focus right
bindsym $mod+Left   focus left
bindsym $mod+Down   focus down
bindsym $mod+Up     focus up
bindsym $mod+Right  focus right

bindsym $mod+Shift+$left  move left
bindsym $mod+Shift+$down  move down
bindsym $mod+Shift+$up    move up
bindsym $mod+Shift+$right move right

# Across monitors. Adding Ctrl re-scopes the same direction keys from
# "within this workspace" to "across outputs":
#   $mod+Ctrl+h/l        move my focus to the next screen
#   $mod+Ctrl+Shift+h/l  take the focused window with me
# Left/right only — the three screens are arranged purely horizontally
# (laptop | 1440p | 1080p), so up/down would be dead keys.
# Ctrl is safe here: constraint 1 forbids Alt, and $mod+Ctrl was unused.
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+$left        focus output left
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+$right       focus output right
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+Left         focus output left
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+Right        focus output right

# `move container to output` carries focus along with the window, so this
# leaves you on the screen you just threw it to.
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+Shift+$left  move container to output left
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+Shift+$right move container to output right
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+Shift+Left   move container to output left
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+Shift+Right  move container to output right

# Relocate the WHOLE workspace, not just one window — the useful one after a
# re-dock, when everything came up on the wrong screen. `<` and `>` sit on the
# comma/period keys and read as left/right; both were unbound.
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+comma        move workspace to output left
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+period       move workspace to output right

### Workspaces -------------------------------------------------------------
# Super+1..9 — safe, since tmux's window switching is Alt+1..9.
bindsym $mod+1 workspace number 1
bindsym $mod+2 workspace number 2
bindsym $mod+3 workspace number 3
bindsym $mod+4 workspace number 4
bindsym $mod+5 workspace number 5
bindsym $mod+6 workspace number 6
bindsym $mod+7 workspace number 7
bindsym $mod+8 workspace number 8
bindsym $mod+9 workspace number 9

bindsym $mod+Shift+1 move container to workspace number 1
bindsym $mod+Shift+2 move container to workspace number 2
bindsym $mod+Shift+3 move container to workspace number 3
bindsym $mod+Shift+4 move container to workspace number 4
bindsym $mod+Shift+5 move container to workspace number 5
bindsym $mod+Shift+6 move container to workspace number 6
bindsym $mod+Shift+7 move container to workspace number 7
bindsym $mod+Shift+8 move container to workspace number 8
bindsym $mod+Shift+9 move container to workspace number 9

### Layout ----------------------------------------------------------------
bindsym $mod+b           splith
bindsym $mod+v           splitv
bindsym $mod+s           layout stacking
bindsym $mod+w           layout tabbed
# ⚠ `split tabbed`, NOT bare `split`. `layout toggle` takes a LIST and cycles
# through it; with only `split` it flips splith <-> splitv, which is a different
# and much less useful thing than what this key looks like it should do. With
# tabbed in the list, one key flips the focused container between the tiling you
# built and one-window-fills-it, and back, with the arrangement intact.
# This is the bar-preserving monocle: unlike $mod+m below it keeps waybar
# visible, at the cost of the tab title row (which sway cannot hide — neither
# `default_border none` nor `hide_edge_borders` touch it).
bindsym $mod+t           layout toggle split tabbed
bindsym $mod+f           fullscreen

# dwm-style monocle for the focused workspace: the focused window fills the
# screen and the focus keys cycle which one is showing, with the tiling
# underneath left completely untouched. Per workspace, so ws1 can be in monocle
# while ws2 is tiled.
#
# ⚠ THIS COVERS WAYBAR, and that is sway's layer ordering rather than a choice
# here — the bar is `layer: top` and sway renders fullscreen surfaces above that
# layer. Measured: a fullscreen window's rect is 0,120 1920x1200, the whole
# output, against 10,172 for a tiled one. $mod+t above is the variant that keeps
# the bar.
#
# ⚠ Absolute path for the same GDM/PATH reason as the brightness keys: sway is
# started without a login shell, so ~/.local/bin is not on its PATH.
# Why this needs a program at all — every focus command is a no-op while a
# window is fullscreen — is measured and written up in config/sway/sway-monocle.
bindsym $mod+m           exec $HOME/.local/bin/sway-monocle toggle
bindsym $mod+Shift+space floating toggle
bindsym $mod+space       focus mode_toggle
bindsym $mod+a           focus parent

# Scratchpad
bindsym $mod+Shift+minus move scratchpad
bindsym $mod+minus       scratchpad show

mode "resize" {
    bindsym $left  resize shrink width  10px
    bindsym $down  resize grow   height 10px
    bindsym $up    resize shrink height 10px
    bindsym $right resize grow   width  10px
    bindsym Return mode "default"
    bindsym Escape mode "default"
}
bindsym $mod+r mode "resize"

### Session ---------------------------------------------------------------
bindsym $mod+Shift+c reload
bindsym $mod+Shift+e exec swaynag -t warning \
    -m 'Exit sway?' -B 'Yes' 'swaymsg exit'
bindsym $mod+Shift+x exec $lock

### Notifications ---------------------------------------------------------
# The keyboard route to swaync's control center; the bell in waybar is the
# pointer one (see "custom/notification" in config/waybar/config). Before these
# two existed the panel had no opener at all, so a notification that timed out
# was simply unrecoverable — the history was being kept and never shown.
#
# -sw ("skip wait") makes the client start swaync if it is not running rather
# than blocking; without it this key does nothing at all on the rare occasion
# the daemon has died, with no feedback.
bindsym $mod+n       exec swaync-client -t -sw
# Push the newest popup off screen without opening the panel — it stays in the
# control center, so this is "not now", not "delete". swaync has no hide-all,
# only hide-latest, so repeated presses peel them off one at a time. Do NOT
# reach for -C here: that is close-all, which drops them from the history too.
bindsym $mod+Shift+n exec swaync-client --hide-latest -sw

### Media / laptop keys ---------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ Each of these is written as `swayosd-client ... || <fallback>` on purpose.
# swayosd draws the on-screen level overlay, and it is what makes the bar's
# missing volume/brightness percentages a feature rather than a loss: the
# number appears center-screen exactly while you are changing it. But this repo
# also configures machines that do not have it, so every key degrades to the
# old direct command instead of silently doing nothing. Test the fallback by
# temporarily renaming swayosd-client, NOT by trusting the `||`.
#
# ⚠ Do NOT drop the `sh -c`. sway execs the command through /bin/sh already,
# but `||` inside a bindsym is parsed by sway's own tokenizer first; wrapping
# it keeps the shell operator with the shell.
bindsym --locked XF86AudioRaiseVolume  exec sh -c 'swayosd-client --output-volume raise  || wpctl set-volume @DEFAULT_AUDIO_SINK@ 5%+'
bindsym --locked XF86AudioLowerVolume  exec sh -c 'swayosd-client --output-volume lower  || wpctl set-volume @DEFAULT_AUDIO_SINK@ 5%-'
bindsym --locked XF86AudioMute         exec sh -c 'swayosd-client --output-volume mute-toggle || wpctl set-mute @DEFAULT_AUDIO_SINK@ toggle'
bindsym --locked XF86AudioMicMute      exec sh -c 'swayosd-client --input-volume mute-toggle  || wpctl set-mute @DEFAULT_AUDIO_SOURCE@ toggle'
# ⚠ Brightness does NOT go through swayosd-client directly, unlike volume.
# swayosd 0.1.0 (Debian trixie) has no minimum and will drive the backlight to
# 0 — a black screen you then have to fix by feel. sway-brightness owns both
# the floor and the 0-100 user scale; see its header. Absolute path because GDM
# starts sway without a login shell, so ~/.local/bin is not on PATH.
bindsym --locked XF86MonBrightnessUp   exec $HOME/.local/bin/sway-brightness up
bindsym --locked XF86MonBrightnessDown exec $HOME/.local/bin/sway-brightness down
bindsym --locked XF86AudioPlay         exec playerctl play-pause
bindsym --locked XF86AudioNext         exec playerctl next
bindsym --locked XF86AudioPrev         exec playerctl previous

### Screenshots -----------------------------------------------------------
bindsym Print       exec grim -g "$(slurp)" - | wl-copy
bindsym Shift+Print exec grim - | wl-copy

### Window rules ----------------------------------------------------------
# Float dialogs rather than tiling them — mouse-friendliness again.
for_window [window_role="dialog"]        floating enable
for_window [window_role="pop-up"]        floating enable
for_window [window_type="dialog"]        floating enable
for_window [app_id="pavucontrol"]        floating enable
# Volume + brightness quick panel, opened by left-clicking those readouts in
# waybar. It replaces waybar's own in-bar sliders, which were unusable — see
# the WHY THIS EXISTS block in config/sway/sway-quickpanel.
for_window [app_id="sway-quickpanel"]    floating enable
for_window [app_id="sway-quickpanel"]    border pixel 2
for_window [app_id="sway-quickpanel"]    sticky enable
# ⚠ sway-calendar DELIBERATELY HAS NO for_window RULES, and adding them is a
# no-op rather than an error. It is a zwlr_layer_shell_v1 surface (like waybar
# and swaync), not an xdg_toplevel, so it never appears in the window tree and no
# `[app_id="sway-calendar"]` criteria can ever match it — floating/border/sticky
# are all meaningless for it, and it gets no client.focused border at all.
#
# It began as a plain toplevel with those three rules, exactly like the
# quickpanel above. The reason it moved: a Wayland client cannot position its own
# toplevel, so sway placed it mid-screen and a `swaymsg move absolute position`
# dragged it under the clock a frame later — VISIBLY. A layer surface takes its
# position from the initial configure, so there is nothing to move. Full write-up
# in docs/sway.md and the script's docstring.
for_window [app_id="blueman-manager"]    floating enable
for_window [app_id="nm-connection-editor"] floating enable

### Idle / lock -----------------------------------------------------------
exec swayidle -w \
    timeout 600  "$lock" \
    timeout 900  'swaymsg "output * power off"' \
        resume   'swaymsg "output * power on"' \
    before-sleep "$lock"

# Lid close → lock (not suspend; logind still handles suspend policy).
bindswitch --reload --locked lid:on exec $lock

### Autostart ------------------------------------------------------------
# Reload-SAFE autostart. Do NOT go back to the pattern of a separate
# `exec_always pkill -x <app>` line followed by `exec_always <app>`: sway forks
# exec lines ASYNCHRONOUSLY, so the pkill races the launch and kills the
# just-started instance. That pattern drops waybar on EVERY `swaymsg reload`
# and intermittently on fresh login. Merging into one `pkill -x waybar; waybar`
# does NOT fix it either — killing waybar and relaunching within one command
# loses the bar at any delay (0/0.5/1s all tested) on a DisplayLink
# primary-GPU-scanout setup. The rule that works: reload the RUNNING instance
# in place, and only spawn one if none is running. Never kill-and-respawn.
# (`||` and `>` below are handled by sh -c, not sway's parser.)
# Supervised by systemd since 2026-09-06, and the rule above still holds.
# `reload-or-restart` SIGUSR2s the RUNNING instance in place via the unit's
# ExecReload (verified: the pid does not change) and only starts one if none is
# running, so this is still "reload in place, never kill-and-respawn".
# What it adds is Restart=on-failure: waybar 0.12.0 segfaults in libglibmm, a
# use-after-free on a Glib::Dispatcher, roughly once a fortnight. As a bare
# exec_always child nothing brought it back, so on 2026-09-05 the bar died at
# 20:21:10 and was gone for 21 hours. It now returns in about a second, and the
# crash is logged as status=11/SEGV in the user journal, which is readable
# WITHOUT the root access the kernel log needs.
# The unit is a full replacement at ~/.config/systemd/user/waybar.service, not a
# drop-in; that file explains why.
exec_always systemctl --user reload-or-restart waybar.service

# SwayFX visual effects — blur, rounded corners, shadows, animations.
#
# ⚠ THE EFFECTS ARE NOT DIRECTIVES IN THIS FILE, AND MUST NOT BECOME ONE. This
# config is shared by all three GDM sessions, and plain sway does not ignore an
# unknown command — it fails the whole config load ("Error(s) loading config!",
# exit 1) and greets you with a swaynag. The fallback session is the recovery
# path; it must stay clean. The script applies everything over IPC and no-ops
# silently when the compositor is not SwayFX. Full reasoning in its header.
#
# ⚠ Absolute via $HOME — GDM starts sway without a login shell, so ~/.local/bin
# is not on the session PATH. Third time this repo has hit that.
#
# exec_always, deliberately: `swaymsg reload` resets runtime-applied effects
# back to what this file says (i.e. none), so re-running here is what makes a
# reload the correct way to re-apply after editing the script.
exec_always $HOME/.local/bin/sway-fx
# The animated workspace row behind waybar's left pill. Owns the single sway
# subscription and feeds nine `tail -F` clients — see the header of
# config/sway/sway-workspaces for why it is a server rather than nine
# subscribers (27 processes on the dock, one per module per bar).
#
# ⚠ NO pgrep GUARD — `pgrep -f sway-workspaces` matches the `sh -c` running
# this very line, the same self-match trap recorded at `exec autotiling` and
# sway-calendar. The script guards itself with a pidfile and exits 0 if a live
# server already owns the row, which is what makes it safe under exec_always
# re-running on every `swaymsg reload`.
exec_always sh -c '[ -x "$HOME/.local/bin/sway-workspaces" ] && "$HOME/.local/bin/sway-workspaces" &'

# swaync, not mako: mako 1.10 has no fade/animation/opacity option of any kind,
# so notifications could only blink out instantly. swaync animates them
# (transition-time in config/swaync/config.json).
# ⚠ THE CONDITION IS `pgrep`, NOT THE RELOAD'S EXIT CODE. This line used to read
#     if swaync-client --reload-config; then ... else swaync; fi
# on the assumption that the reload FAILS when no daemon is running, so the
# `else` would launch one. It does not fail — it BLOCKS, printing
#     Could not connect to CC service. Will wait for connection...
# and waits forever for a daemon that nothing is left to start. So on any
# session where swaync was not already up, this line hung at login and swaync
# never started at all: no control center, and the waybar bell silently doing
# nothing (its `-sw` gives up rather than launching — see config/waybar/config).
# Observed live: the hung `swaync-client --reload-config` was still sitting
# there 24 minutes into the session, sway's exec_always never having returned.
#
# Waiting is the documented default; `-sw`/`--skip-wait` is opt-out. So decide
# on whether the process exists, exactly as the swayosd line below does, and
# never on what the client returns.
#
# ⚠ `-sw` MUST FOLLOW THE ACTION FLAG, not precede it. swaync-client takes one
# option: `swaync-client -sw --reload-config` prints its usage and exits **0**,
# which looks like success and reloads nothing. `--reload-config -sw` is right.
#
# ⚠ Still if/then/else, NOT `A && B || swaync`: with that precedence a
# successful --reload-config followed by a FAILED --reload-css (an invalid
# stylesheet) falls through to `||` and spawns a SECOND daemon alongside the
# running one.
# ⚠ mako is still INSTALLED as a fallback and its config is still managed. To
# go back, swap this line for:  exec_always makoctl reload || mako
exec_always sh -c 'command -v swaync >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0; if pgrep -x swaync >/dev/null 2>&1; then swaync-client --reload-config -sw && swaync-client --reload-css -sw; else swaync & fi'

# swayosd — the on-screen volume/brightness/caps-lock overlay the media keys
# above drive. Optional: absent binary means the keys fall back to wpctl and
# brightnessctl, so this line no-ops rather than erroring.
#
# ⚠ Guarded with pgrep rather than the pkill-and-relaunch shape used for
# waybar. This is exec_always, so it re-runs on every `swaymsg reload`, and
# swayosd-server holds the layer-shell surface — restarting it on each reload
# would flash the overlay away mid-use for no benefit. Start-if-absent is
# enough because the server is stateless.
exec_always sh -c 'command -v swayosd-server >/dev/null 2>&1 && ! pgrep -x swayosd-server >/dev/null && swayosd-server &'

# sway-calendar as a RESIDENT SERVER, started hidden. Clicking the clock in
# waybar signals it (SIGUSR1) to toggle, which costs ~65ms instead of the ~366ms
# a fresh Python+GTK process needs — measured, and 260ms of that is fixed
# interpreter and GTK init before any of the script's own code runs, so
# spawn-per-click cannot be made to feel responsive.
#
# ⚠ `--anchor` MUST MATCH WHERE THE CLOCK IS in config/waybar/config, because the
# SERVER owns the geometry now — the click path only delivers a signal and cannot
# pass an anchor. Clock in modules-center -> `--anchor center`.
#
# ⚠ NO pgrep GUARD, deliberately, and do not add one. `pgrep -f sway-calendar`
# would match the `sh -c` running this very line — the same self-match trap the
# autotiling comment above records — so it could never fire. The script guards
# itself instead: `--server` checks its own pidfile and declines to start a second
# resident instance, which is what makes this safe under `exec_always` re-running
# on every `swaymsg reload`. Absolute path for the same GDM/PATH reason as the
# brightness keys.
exec_always sh -c '[ -x "$HOME/.local/bin/sway-calendar" ] && "$HOME/.local/bin/sway-calendar" --server --anchor center &'

# sway-monocle's resident half, bound to $mod+m above. It exists for the events
# a keybinding cannot see: a window OPENED while monocle is on takes focus but
# is not fullscreen, so it would appear behind the one that still is, and
# CLOSING the visible window drops you back to the tiling underneath. The daemon
# re-shows whatever sway settles focus on, which covers both.
# ⚠ NO pgrep GUARD, for the same self-match reason as sway-calendar and
# sway-workspaces above — `pgrep -f sway-monocle` matches this very `sh -c`.
# `--server` checks its own pidfile and declines to start a second instance,
# which is what makes this safe under `exec_always` on every reload.
# ⚠ It also clears its state on startup, so a reload can never leave a
# workspace marked monocle with nothing actually fullscreen.
exec_always sh -c '[ -x "$HOME/.local/bin/sway-monocle" ] && "$HOME/.local/bin/sway-monocle" --server &'

# Dynamic-tiling approximation over sway IPC — the mitigation for sway being
# manual-tiling. Comment this out to judge plain sway.
# Plain `exec` (NOT exec_always): start one autotiling at login and leave it.
# It has no config to reload, so re-running it on every `swaymsg reload` only
# leaked duplicates. Two dead-ends that look tempting: `exec_always pkill -x
# autotiling; autotiling` never matches (autotiling runs as comm `python3`, so
# `pgrep -x autotiling` reports it absent while it runs), and a `pgrep -f
# /usr/bin/autotiling || autotiling` guard SELF-MATCHES the sh running it (that
# cmdline contains the path), so it would never launch on a fresh login.
exec autotiling

# Polkit agent — REQUIRED. Without it GUI privilege prompts silently fail.
# On Debian the path is /usr/libexec/, NOT /usr/lib/mate-polkit/. Confirm on
# another distro with:  dpkg -L mate-polkit | grep authentication-agent
# No lxpolkit fallback — a fallback to an uninstalled binary just masks a failure.
exec /usr/libexec/polkit-mate-authentication-agent-1

# The tray broker. ⚠ START IT EARLY AND DO NOT MAKE IT CONDITIONAL.
#
# This owns the session's org.kde.StatusNotifierWatcher and hands waybar the
# item list minus nm-applet's permanently-Active network icon, which is the
# only reason the bar can carry a tray at all. Its own header has the full
# reasoning; two things matter at this level:
#
# ⚠ THE WATCHER MUST EXIST BEFORE A TRAY APP STARTS, and "before" means
# before the app, not before waybar. Measured: an app already registered
# re-registers happily when the watcher changes hands, but Chromium (so every
# Electron app, so Discord) decides ONCE at tray-icon creation — launched with
# no watcher on the bus it falls back to X11 XEmbed, which does not exist here,
# and never gets an icon however long it runs. Nothing reports this; the icon
# is simply absent.
#
# ⚠ NO pgrep GUARD, and none is needed — the same self-match trap as
# sway-calendar and sway-workspaces applies, and here the D-Bus name IS the
# guard: a second copy cannot take the name (it deliberately does not ask for
# ALLOW_REPLACEMENT), sees that it is not the owner and exits 0. That is what
# makes this safe under exec_always. It is exec_always rather than exec so a
# `swaymsg reload` revives it if it ever died.
exec_always sh -c '[ -x "$HOME/.local/bin/sway-tray-filter" ] && "$HOME/.local/bin/sway-tray-filter" &'

# Tray applets.
#
# ⚠ nm-applet's icon is HIDDEN by sway-tray-filter above, and this line still
# has to stay. Its real job here is being NetworkManager's secret agent — the
# thing that prompts for a wifi password when you join a new network — which it
# does over D-Bus with or without a visible icon. Removing the line would
# silently stop those prompts. Do not "simplify" the filter away by killing
# this instead.
# Note that neither dropping --indicator nor
# `gsettings set org.gnome.nm-applet show-applet false` suppresses its
# StatusNotifier item; both still register it as Active (retested 2026-08-12).
exec nm-applet --indicator
# blueman-applet registers no StatusNotifier item at all, so it has never been
# in the tray and nothing changed for it — it still runs and still handles
# pairing, and blueman-manager is reached from the swaync panel.
exec blueman-applet

# Hand the session env to the portals / systemd user units.
exec systemctl --user import-environment WAYLAND_DISPLAY XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP
exec dbus-update-activation-environment --systemd WAYLAND_DISPLAY XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=sway

# Dark appearance for GTK4/libadwaita apps.
#
# ⚠ THIS IS THE HALF THAT config/gtk/*-settings.ini CANNOT DO, and both halves
# are needed. libadwaita ignores settings.ini for light/dark entirely: it asks
# the xdg-desktop-portal Settings interface for org.freedesktop.appearance
# color-scheme. Under sway that interface is served by xdg-desktop-portal-gtk
# (routed explicitly in config/sway/sway-portals.conf), and the GTK backend
# answers it by reading exactly this GSettings key. So without this line a
# libadwaita app opens light no matter what the ini files say. GTK3 apps are the
# reverse case — they read the ini and ignore this. Hence one of each.
#
# ⚠ GSettings IS USER-GLOBAL, NOT SESSION-SCOPED — this is the one thing in this
# file that outlives the sway session. It writes to dconf, so GNOME picks up the
# same preference on the next login and stays dark until something changes it
# back. That is a deliberate accepted side effect (a dark GNOME is wanted too),
# but it does mean this is NOT of a piece with sway-portals.conf, which really is
# sway-only. Undo with:
#     gsettings set org.gnome.desktop.interface color-scheme 'default'
#
# exec_always, not exec: cheap, idempotent, and it re-asserts the value after
# anything else (a GNOME login, a stray `gsettings reset`) has changed it.
exec_always gsettings set org.gnome.desktop.interface color-scheme 'prefer-dark'

# swaybar is replaced by waybar; keep this empty block out of the config
# rather than defining an unused `bar {}`.

include /etc/sway/config.d/*
